Individual Case Safety Report (ICSR) Assessment SimulationRole: Drug Safety Associate (DSA) / Safety Evaluation SpecialistRegulatory Standard: ICH E2A, ICH E2B(R3), ICH E2D, MedDRA v27.0, and WHO-UMC Guidelines1. Source Case Narrative (Intake)Case ID: PV-2026-IND-00841Source: Spontaneous report received from a practicing Pulmonologist (Hospital-based HCP).Patient Details: 54-year-old male (Initials: R.K.), Weight: 68 kg, Height: 172 cm.Medical History: Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (managed for 6 years), Moderate Persistent Bronchial Asthma (12 years). No prior history of cardiac disease, hepatic impairment, or drug allergies.Concomitant Medications: Metformin 500 mg PO BID (ongoing), Inhaled Budesonide/Formoterol 200/6 mcg 2 puffs BID (ongoing).Suspected Drug: Moxifloxacin 400 mg tablets (Brand: Avalox/Generic equivalent), Lot # MX2509, Expiry: 11/2027.Indication: Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP).Dosing Regimen: 400 mg orally once daily, started on 12-Sep-2026.Event Description: On 15-Sep-2026 (Day 4 of therapy), the patient experienced sudden-onset palpitations, lightheadedness, and syncope at home. He was brought to the Emergency Department. 12-lead ECG revealed a marked prolongation of the corrected QT interval (QTc = 512 ms; baseline ECG prior to admission was 410 ms) with short runs of non-sustained polymorphic ventricular tachycardia consistent with Torsades de Pointes (TdP). Moxifloxacin was immediately withdrawn. Serum potassium was 3.8 mEq/L (mildly low/borderline) and magnesium was 1.9 mg/dL. The patient was admitted to the Cardiac Care Unit (CCU), administered IV magnesium sulfate (2 g over 15 min), and kept on continuous telemetry. By 18-Sep-2026, the QTc normalized to 425 ms without further arrhythmic events. The patient was discharged in stable condition on 19-Sep-2026. Rechallenge was not performed.2. Primary Triage & Core Four Minimum Criteria (ICH E2D / E2B)To qualify as a valid ICSR for regulatory transmission, the report must satisfy four core data 