nationReference Safety Information (RSI): Approved Summary of Product Characteristics (SmPC) / Prescribing Information for Moxifloxacin 400 mg.Evaluation:Moxifloxacin has an established class warning regarding inhibition of cardiac voltage-gated potassium channels ($I_{Kr}$ / HERG channel), leading to concentration-dependent QT interval prolongation and risks of ventricular arrhythmias including Torsades de Pointes.The event is explicitly listed in Section 4.4 (Special warnings and precautions) and Section 4.8 (Undesirable effects) of the product label.Determination: Expected (Listed).6. Causality AssessmentMethod 1: Naranjo Algorithm (Probability Scale)QuestionEvaluation / ResponseScore1. Are there previous conclusive reports on this reaction?Yes (well-documented class effect of fluoroquinolones)+12. Did the adverse event appear after the suspected drug was administered?Yes (occurred on Day 4 of therapy)+23. Did the adverse event improve when the drug was discontinued?Yes (positive dechallenge: QTc normalized to 425 ms by 18-Sep)+14. Did the adverse reaction reappear when the drug was readministered?Not done / Not applicable05. Are there alternate causes that could solely explain the reaction?No (baseline ECG was normal; mild hypokalemia alone does not account for acute TdP without drug interaction)+26. Did the reaction appear when a placebo was given?No / Unknown07. Was the drug detected in blood/body fluids in toxic concentrations?Drug concentration not measured08. Was the reaction more severe when dose increased / less severe when decreased?Unknown (drug was completely halted)09. Did the patient have a similar reaction to the same or similar drugs in the past?No / Negative history010. Was the adverse event confirmed by objective evidence?Yes (12-lead ECG documentation: QTc = 512 ms, telemetry confirmation of TdP)+1Total Naranjo Score+7Naranjo Conclusion: Score 7 correlates to a PROBABLE causal association (Score range: $\ge 9$ = Definite; 5–8 = Probable; 1–4 = Possible; 0 = Doubtful).Method 2: WHO-UMC Causality ScaleAssessment Category: PROBABLE / LIKELYCriteria Satisfied:Plausible temporal sequence (onset 3 days post-initiation).Reasonable response to withdrawal (positive dechallenge: normalized ECG within 72 hours of cessation).Objective laboratory/electrophysiological documentation of the event.Pharmacological plausibility (known $I_{Kr}$ blocker).The event cannot reasonably be attributed to the patient’s underlying conditions (asthma, diabetes) or concomitant medications (metformin, budesonide/formoterol).7. Regulatory Reporting Timelines & PathwayReport Type: Post-Marketing Spontaneous ICSR.Seriousness & Expectedness Combination: Serious + Expected.Expedited Clock Determination:Under standard ICH E2D / US FDA 21 CFR 314.80, mandatory 15-day expedited reporting applies to Serious and Unexpected events.However, under EMA GVP Module VI and certain strict health authority jurisdictions (including severe life-threatening cardiac events flagged as IMEs), Serious Expected post-marketing reports are processed as follows:Expedited Submission: Transmitted within 15 calendar days of receipt (Day 0 = Date HCP reported the case to the Marketing Authorization Holder).Periodic Collation: Integrated into the next scheduled Periodic Benefit-Risk Evaluation Report (PBRER) / Periodic Safety Update Report (PSUR).Format: ICH E2B(R3) XML transmitted to relevant regulatory gateways (e.g., US FDA FAERS, EMA EudraVigilance, CDSCO PvPI).8. Drug Safety Associate (DSA) Medical Evaluation & Risk ManagementDechallenge & Rechallenge Action:Dechallenge: Positive. The event resolved completely post-discontinuation without permanent sequelae.Rechallenge: Negative (not attempted due to life-threatening risk). Moxifloxacin should be flagged as permanently contraindicated in this patient's medical records.Contributing / Confounding Factors:Serum potassium was borderline low (3.8 mEq/L), which reduces cardiac repolarization reserve and potentiates fluoroquinolone-induced QT prolongation.Formoterol (inhaled $\beta_2$-agonist) can occasionally cause mild hypokalemia and slight arrhythmogenic risk, representing a potential minor pharmacodynamic interaction, though moxifloxacin is the dominant trigger.Follow-Up Query (Targeted Questionnaire):Request confirmation of any other over-the-counter or herbal remedies taken prior to onset.Obtain discharge summary and copies of the ECG strips for electronic file archiving.Confirm that no family history of congenital Long QT Syndrome (LQTS) exists.Aggregate Safety Impact:Event aligns with the established risk profile; no immediate product label update or safety signal escalation is warranted based on this single case.Case will be indexed under the company's designated Safety Concern: Cardiac Arrhythmia / QTc Prolongation for cumulative disproportionality surveillance (PRR/ROR monitoring).
